  "title": "Is the squared signal of the F_q[T] probe an intersection-form square",
  "path": "push/c10-ff-signal-intersection-square",
  "note": "F_q[T], prime cubic conductor, N <= 3, q = 5, 7, 11, exact. For genus-1 targets the Moebius sum is S_cop(k) = sum_j c_j t_j with c independent of the curve, and (sum c_j t_j)^2 = -D.D/(2 q^N) for every real quadratic 1 - a u + q u^2, on or off the circle: the equality is deg[m] = m^2 on an elliptic curve, uses no index theorem and carries no positivity. Fakes with off-line zeros pass (3444 of 3444). In genus 2 the equality fails (0 of 80; 0 of 624) and the index theorem bounds the squared signal from above (the Weil bound), the opposite side from the low input. M_N = h_N is not trace-linear fo",
  "lesson": "In genus 1 every symmetric function of the Frobenius eigenvalues is linear in the traces, so any squared signal is trivially an intersection-form square (deg[m] = m^2) and the test separates nothing: fakes with off-line zeros and the Liouville-twisted target pass. The Hodge index theorem acts on the squared signal only as an upper bound (Weil bound for the target), the opposite side from what the low input needs. Model targets must have genus >= 2 before a \"square\" identity carries positivity content; there the Moebius signal is the trace on Sym^N H^1 with 1/L' weights, not a Weil functional.",
  "refereeHolds": true,
  "aboutZeros": false
 },
