  "note": "Control lane, no attack. Relative to a classical short-interval prime theorem (counting form, one theta < 1) and with the referee's repair of Lemma 0: deleting a greedy prime set S of density t^(b-1)/log t (density one, not one half) gives F = zeta(s)(s - b)W(s) with Lambda_F >= 0, coefficients 0/1, a simple pole at 1 and a simple zero at b. Limits: the zero comes from the theorem, not from data (every finite truncation is positive at b; finite data do not enclose the zero); the F'(b+) enclosures are relative to two cited explicit pi-li bounds; the device read (14 pass, 20 do not) is argued, n",
  "lesson": "The zero of a control built from an infinite prime set comes from the theorem, not the data: every finite truncation is positive at b, and an enclosure left of b needs an explicit Hoheisel-type bound at all scales (explicit power-saving bounds stop near 1.4e25). Positivity plus unimodular parameters at every rational prime identifies zeta, so THIN and the deletion control together cover every degree-one Euler-product read set without the functional equation or periodicity. The devices the control does not pass read only the two known sets (functional equation; periodicity with unimodular parameters), an attached object, or positivity with zeta's gamma side.",
