  "title": "Power-saving discrepancy of a completely multiplicative unimodular chi versus a zero-free region",
  "path": "push/c4-helson-discrepancy-zerofree",
  "note": "Landau's argument on H = F F*/zeta^2 = exp(-2A), A a non-negative Dirichlet series, gives ord_F(s) + ord_F(conj s) = 2 ord_zeta(s) on Re s > sigma_0 (sigma_0 = top real zero of F in (theta,1), or theta): the hypothesis compares F with zeta and nothing else, so a zero-free statement for F right of sigma_0 is the same statement for zeta (circular). The proof does not use the additive spacing of the integers and transfers to Beurling systems. Real zeros of F unrelated to zeta can lie right of theta (measured: greedy thin twists have a double zero at b with discrepancy exponent near b/2), so f(the",
  "lesson": "Power-saving discrepancy plus Euler product compares F with zeta and nothing more: the proof never uses the additive spacing of the integers and passes the Beurling control unchanged. r1 is true only for this relative theorem (Euler product gives the non-negative series A; DH fails there), not for any conclusion about zeta. The planner's expectation that a thin twist has discrepancy exponent b was wrong: measured about b/2, so sigma_0 > theta on the evidence (not proved; a proof needs prime exponential sums over S). c3's bounded-discrepancy citation is corrected to Klurman-Mangerel-Pohoata-Teravainen, arXiv:1911.06265 (abstract only read).",
  "refereeHolds": true,
  "aboutZeros": false
 },
