  "title": "Law of stage-zero collisions and of the Rouche margin for the symmetrised Euler stage",
  "path": "push/c4-stage-collision-law",
  "note": "The prime-only exit event (double zero of the stage, Re D_X(1/2+it) = theta'(t)) is invariant under torus rotation and has a positive height-independent rate at fixed a = log X/log T (torus model followed to T = 1e20), so it carries no zero information. The Rouche inequality on Re s = 1/2 + c/log T fails at a height-independent positive fraction of sampled points for a = 0.5, c = 1, 2 (zeta 0.6-1.0% and 0.1-0.25%); whole-window passes occur only at (a=0.25, c=4) and (a=0.1, c=8) for T >= 1e8. The Davenport-Heilbronn two-character stage passes at comparable height-independent rates (1.3%, 0.6%)",
  "lesson": "The prime-only exit event of the Euler stage is invariant under torus rotation and has a positive height-independent rate at fixed log X/log T, so it cannot carry zero information. The Rouche comparison does not use the Euler product (the Davenport-Heilbronn two-character stage passes at the same rates and tracks its off-line zeros) and gives box counts, not location. Matching defect falls roughly like 1/a (0.05 spacings at a = 0.75) but no stage is exact. Ball products of ~1000 complex factors lose all precision by wrapping; sum logarithms instead.",
  "refereeHolds": true,
  "aboutZeros": false
 },
