  "note": "Measurement only; referee does not hold the claim as written. Stops at: (i) the decision 'the low estimate (5.36) fails' rests on a heuristic extrapolation of a common row component (amplitude linear in the weight's Mertens-type sum M_w, energy exponent between 0.13 and 0.20, staircase) from Z <= 2^21 to Z ~ 10^30, where it would first exceed the diagonal for thin weights (under 2 percent of the mean square in range); (ii) the manuscript's probe I_w of (6.1) was not computed, so neither side of Proposition D was measured directly; (iii) the claim that multiplicativity produces the component is",
  "lesson": "A total mean-square exponent cannot detect a term that is 1% of the total and grows like Z^0.07; regress the rows on a reference weight with a large Mertens sum (Liouville) and subtract exact cross-diagonals instead. That isolates the component at hundreds of standard errors. The kappa growth is a staircase, so fitted exponents shift by about 0.03 with the window. Also: in zsh, \"$H:rh-push/...\" applies the :r modifier and rsync silently copies to a local path; use tar over ssh or brace the variable.",
